text:
Maya Chen: Hey team — quick question on hosted API keys for dev/demo. I'm doing a live demo tomorrow and planning to run it from my laptop. Is it ok to use our "hosted" project key on my local machine for the demo, or should I request something else?

Alex Rivera: short answer: don't check the key into code or public places. using it locally for a private demo is fine, but prefer ephemeral/demo tokens when possible. also be aware of quota/rate buckets. :slightly_smiling_face: 

Maya Chen: thanks. what are the rate buckets? i hit 429s yesterday when streaming multiple demos concurrently. i saw this response: 
```
{\n  "error": "rate_limit_exceeded",\n  "message": "Too many requests",\n  "retry_after": 12\n}
```

Sam Ortiz: That looks like the typical per-key burst window. We have: per-key concurrent streams, per-project token-per-minute, and per-route limits (e.g., chat-streaming). For demos, a quick workaround is to stagger start times or reduce parallelism. 

Priya Shah: If you need to run several parallel sessions for the demo, ping ops (or request a temporary local bump). We can enable a short-lived bump tied to your email or machine IP for the demo window. 

Maya Chen: How do I request that bump? any form or just @ops?

ops-bot: To request a temporary demo bump, file a Support ticket: https://redwood.internal/support/new with subject "Demo quota bump" and include: 1) root email or team, 2) desired start/end time, 3) approximate RPS or concurrent streams, 4) IPs if using IP allowlist. Ops will respond within 2 business hours.

Alex Rivera: Also consider creating an ephemeral key via the console (Console -> API Keys -> Create ephemeral key) if you want programmatic tokens that expire. Example to generate a demo token from console: 
```
# in console UI: Create Key -> Mode: Ephemeral -> TTL: 4h
```
This avoids long-lived keys on your laptop.

Maya Chen: awesome. one more: our team sometimes shares a single hosted key across devs behind an internal proxy. safe?

infra-eng: Sharing is okay if the key is kept within an internal vault/proxy and not pushed to repos. If you do share, make sure the proxy sets an "X-Forwarded-For" or our allowed IP headers and that logs don't accidentally leak the token. We also recommend scoping the key to least-privilege (embeddings vs chat-only) and rotating monthly.

Sam Ortiz: FYI, the Retry-After header is honored. If you get a 429, exponential backoff + jitter works well. Example header: `Retry-After: 12` (seconds). Also check the response body for which bucket triggered the limit.

Maya Chen: thanks all — will file the support ticket and use ephemeral key for the laptop. I'll avoid committing anything. any quick snippet to show streaming reconnect/backoff during the demo?

Alex Rivera: quick JS pseudocode: 
```
async function fetchWithBackoff(url, opts) {
  let retry = 0;
  while (retry < 5) {
    const res = await fetch(url, opts);
    if (res.status !== 429) return res;
    const wait = (2 ** retry) * 1000 + Math.random() * 300;
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
    retry++;
  }
  throw new Error('failed after retries');
}
```

Priya Shah: And if you need IP allowlisting for the demo: include your laptop/public IP in the support ticket and we'll add it to the temporary allowlist.

Maya Chen: perfect — filing ticket now. thanks! :pray:

ops-bot: Ticket received: #SUP-4721. Ops will review and reply with ETA. Please do not share the key publicly.
